      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can frame the lesson around the concept of \"community vs. commerce\" by highlighting the historical roots of urban planning in the United States, particularly the Gilded Age and the robber barons who prioritized private development over public spaces. This era of unchecked capitalism led to the creation of sprawling, car-dependent cities that isolated low-income communities and perpetuated systemic inequality. In contrast, the modern movement towards inclusive, human-centric community spaces is rooted in the progressive ideals of the 1960s and 1970s, which emphasized the importance of public spaces for social cohesion and community building. By exploring these two eras, students can gain a deeper understanding of the ongoing struggle for equitable and just urban planning.",
